\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Bondi's sign rules for two bodies released at rest, in the style of Forward's analysis~\cite  {forward1990}. Every body here has positive inertial and passive mass; only the \emph  {active} (source) mass carries a sign---negative for the dashed bodies. (a)~Two positive-mass stars attract each other and fall together; (b)~two negative-mass stars repel each other and push apart. In both cases the forces oppose, and the pair barycentre stays put. (c)~The mixed pair---the Bondi dipole: the positive star is repelled by the negative one, the negative star is attracted by the positive one, so both forces point the \emph  {same} way and the pair barycentre accelerates, the system carrying zero total momentum in the signed bookkeeping. Each row is realized as a run of the matrix in Table~\ref {tab:matrix}, and the pair barycentre named in each row is the quantity every later figure measures.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:schematic}{{1}{2}{Bondi's sign rules for two bodies released at rest, in the style of Forward's analysis~\cite {forward1990}. Every body here has positive inertial and passive mass; only the \emph {active} (source) mass carries a sign---negative for the dashed bodies. (a)~Two positive-mass stars attract each other and fall together; (b)~two negative-mass stars repel each other and push apart. In both cases the forces oppose, and the pair barycentre stays put. (c)~The mixed pair---the Bondi dipole: the positive star is repelled by the negative one, the negative star is attracted by the positive one, so both forces point the \emph {same} way and the pair barycentre accelerates, the system carrying zero total momentum in the signed bookkeeping. Each row is realized as a run of the matrix in Table~\ref {tab:matrix}, and the pair barycentre named in each row is the quantity every later figure measures}{figure.1}{}}
